vet  booster shots mandatory to travel freely  within the eu without testing or  quarantine the european commission says  the block's digital covid certificate  will only be valid for nine months after  a person's second dose after that a  booster shot is needed to renew the  certificate the new measure still needs  to be approved by all eu members but  it's expected to come into effect in  february  a british court has ordered the ruler of  dubai to pay his ex-wife and their  children more than 900 million dollars  it's one of the most expensive divorce  settlements in british history sheikh  muhammad bin rasheed al-maktoum was  ordered to pay nearly half of the  settlement to his sixth wife and the  rest to their two daughters the trio  fled to the uk in 2019 the princess  sought custody of their two kids through  british courts saying she was terrified  of her husband  can the highly contagious variant open a  path to herd immunity we're answering  your omicron questions next  anyone looking to make a quick  cross-border christmas shopping trip  will now require a negative covid19 test  before returning home starting today the  federal government is requiring anyone  entering the country to show proof of a  negative pcr test the health minister  announced the new rules on friday citing  the surge in omicron infections before  now travelers re-entering the country  after short trips of less than 72 hours  were not required to take a test  well from testing to treatment we have  received a lot of questions from viewers  about the omicron variant and to answer  some of those now we are joined by  infectious disease expert dr dan gregson  and epidemiologist dr christopher labos  thank you gentlemen both for joining us  so  dr labos first to you to you and we have  a question here from kevin who says that  he's been in contact with a person who  has confirmed positive for omicron how  long before he might also test positive  so you probably won't test positive  during the first few days of the uh  after the exposure so day one and day  two you probably won't but by day three  four and five uh if you are infected you  probably will test positive at that  point and then if you know if you get  too far away from the initial exposure  after about day seven or eight the  likelihood of you testing positive  starts to drop off again okay very good  to know next question comes from joe  with a question about masks and so dr  gregson this one to you uh joe sees  people wearing face shields with no face  masks all the time wondering how  effective these face shields and face  masks are against the omicron variant  short answers they're not um  this is a bit of misinformation that  that face shields are used to prevent  transmission of respiratory infections  that's not what happens we use face  shields in addition to masks  to reduce the risk of transmission so  they're an added feature they're not a  single feature so to speak  okay uh next question comes from lori  this one for you dr labos asking if  there are any treatment options against  omicron and asking specifically about  antibody treatments that are widely  available in the united states  yeah so i think this is a common  misconception there are actually  multiple treatments for covid and  there's no reason to think that the  treatments for omicron would be  different than they would for all the  other variants of covid i mean at its  very basics it's uh steroids which were  one of the first treatments to be used  there are some antivirals  from the severe being one there are the  new oral pills that were developed by  merkin by pfizer but those aren't really  used yet  and there are a number of other  treatments as well the concern has  always been that antibody based  treatments might not be as effective  because of the differences between  omicron and the original strains but all  this to say in brief there are actually  multiple treatments for covid  that can help speed the recovery of  people when they're in hospital but of  course always much better to not get  covet in the first place and that's the  benefit of vaccines you actually prevent  disease rather than treating you after  you get sick  right excellent point of course and back  over to you dr gregson next question  comes from rod this is a common question  actually we've received a lot of asking  about herd immunity so if omicron is  highly contagious and a large percentage  of the population of canada were to  catch it and then recover  would we be able to go back to normal  so  respiratory viruses aren't like some  other viruses we see circulating in the  environment so  um  what generally happens is we have a wave  of virus come through we develop  immunity  the virus then mutates a little bit we  have another wave of virus come through  and we develop immunity  our current problem is we don't large  partisan postulation doesn't have  immunities bringing these big waves  uh and that as we more and more develop  immunity to just different variants  we'll have fewer and fewer  waves and they'll be  not as big as time goes by  okay last question to you dr labos we're  almost out of time but this one from  jaclyn who notes uh with omicron  spreading so rapidly throughout the  world what are your thoughts on the odds  of the virus going through another even  more concerning mutation  well it's certainly possible especially  if we don't control covet globally if we  allow covet to spread and to be  spreading rapidly in various parts of  the world in various countries where  vaccine uptake is low then yes this  could happen again this is how delta  emerged this is how omicron emerged so  unless we know once this wave is over if  we don't realize and remember that we  need to get vaccines to countries where  uh supply of vaccination is low this  probably will keep happening so we have  to remember that we have to control  this virus not just in canada but  control it globally  ugh thanks for nothing on that last one  uh doctors dan gregson christopher lavos  gentlemen really appreciate your time  thanks for tackling these questions for  us  you're welcome have a great day  and we will be putting questions about  omicron straight to the experts all week  so please send us yours to your  questions at globalnews.ca  christmas tree troubles next why  california's climate has uprooted the  supply  welcome back it's hard to imagine the  holidays without one the christmas tree  is a centerpiece for many family  gatherings and it's especially festive  if you find a real one but on the u.s  west coast christmas trees are harder to  come by this year rob malcolm explains  dazzler farms has long been a staple of  the fall season in california growing  pumpkins and this year they are also in  the christmas tree business but with  wildfires and unprecedented drought that  transition hasn't been easy  many west coast sellers who rely on  supply from california and oregon have  had a hard time filling their lots they  don't have the tree supply like they  used to between the fires and the  drought the supply just isn't there  wildfires in 2020 had already wiped out  several christmas tree farms in  california and with unprecedented  drought in 2021 even more farms have  struggled meaning fewer trees overall  and that's a driven price is up  christmas trees are fun way up i mean i  have a lot of clients that are on the  real estate agent like they complain all  the time about the prices this year it's  getting crazy this year alone the cost  of real trees is estimated to be up 20  percent over last year and that's on top  of the increases already felt over the  past few years  while the biggest might be on the price  there's also been an impact on the size  of the trees between fires and drought  combined like this year it was almost  impossible to get tall trees and then a  few years skyrocketing tree prices and  height won't be the only issue that's  because it takes 6 to 10 years to grow a  christmas tree to maturity and when  seedlings have been damaged because of  drought and wildfires the impacts will  be felt for years to come it's not a  dying business but christmas tree farms  like bobby dazzlers just outside of  sacramento understand what's at stake  and they say christmases in the future  could look a little bit differently  we're going to see the effects of this  for years to come for some consumers  switching christmas traditions to  include an artificial tree might be the  answer in the long term but amid supply  chain issues artificial trees have been  in short supply this year too supply and  demand it's on everything so this  christmas real trees may look a little  different and cost more but with  droughts and wildfires becoming more  prevalent in a state already suffering  there are questions about whether  christmas trees will become harder and  harder to come by in the coming years  forcing a switch to artificial for many  consumers rob malcolm global news  sacramento  sorry santa that is global national for  this tuesday night i'm jeff semple today  is the winter solstice an important  event for many cultures including  canada's indigenous peoples so we leave  you tonight with these sculptures in  edmonton by cree artist jason carter  marking the longest night of the year  they represent the grandfather's son the  grandmother moon and mother earth each  holding a light to care for us in the  darkness thanks for watching hope to see  you back here again tomorrow have a  great night  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

